## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of in-place bitboard style transforms, pure bitwise logic, and branchless design for a fixed-width data layout.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-slice permutation across 8x8 matrix
- Masked swaps and shifts to perform transposition in O(1)
- Endianness and layout nullified by a consistent row-major interpretation

## Code Example
```javascript
function transpose8x8(x) {
  // Use BigInt for 64-bit arithmetic in JS
  let t;
  const m1 = 0x5555555555555555n;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 1n)) & m1;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 1n);

  const m2 = 0x3333333333333333n;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 2n)) & m2;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 2n);

  const m4 = 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 4n)) & m4;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 4n);

  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 16x16 on a 128-bit word? needed masks and shifts?
- How would you handle different input mappings (column-major) or endianness concerns?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests per-byte parallel popcount without loops, scales to 128-bit data, and checks careful bit-twiddling across halves.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR per-byte popcount
- 128-bit lane handling via hi/lo halves
- No branches, fixed sequence of masks/shifts

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 countBytes128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.lo;
  uint64_t b = x.hi;
  a = a - ((a >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  a = (a & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((a >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  a = (a + (a >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  b = b - ((b >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  b = (b & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((b >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  b = (b + (b >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint128 r = { .hi = b, .lo = a };
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to count bits in arbitrary 16-byte vectors with fewer operations? 
- What are the effects of endianness on byte ordering in different compilers and architectures?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of cross-limb bit indexing and non-trivial bit-twiddling without branching. It introduces a different angle: packing every other bit across a 128-bit value into a 64-bit result.

## Key Concepts

- **Piece-wise bit extraction with masks:** Isolate target bits using bitwise AND with appropriate masks.
- **In-limb and cross-limb mapping:** Map even bits within each 64-bit limb, then combine results.
- **Parallel bit-pack technique:** Use the classic "compress" trick—mask, shift, and merge repeatedly to collapse bits.
- **Endianness and limb numbering:** The `lo` limb contains bits 0-63, and `hi` contains bits 64-127 (little-endian representation).

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint64_t packEvenBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo;
  lo &= 0x5555555555555555ULL;              // keep even bits
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 1)) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 2)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 4)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 8)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 16)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;

  uint64_t hi = x.hi;
  hi &= 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 1)) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 2)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 4)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 8)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 16)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;

  return (hi << 32) | lo;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Fresh bit-hack that demonstrates mastery of fixed-step SWAR technique and portability across languages.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR fixed-masks and staged swaps
- Bitwise rotation-free reversal
- Unsigned 32-bit wrap handling in JavaScript/TypeScript

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverseBits32(n) {
  n = ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555) | ((n & 0x55555555) << 1);
  n = ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333) | ((n & 0x33333333) << 2);
  n = ((n >>> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F) | ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F) << 4);
  n = ((n >>> 8) & 0x00FF00FF) | ((n & 0x00FF00FF) << 8);
  n = (n >>> 16) | (n << 16);
  return n >>> 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 64-bit integers in languages with 64-bit ops?
- How to verify correctness with quick property tests (e.g., n reversing twice yields n)?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates the ability to implement a classic bit-twiddling technique (popcount) on 128-bit integers without branching. This skill is useful in hash tables, connectivity algorithms, and low-latency code.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting (divide-and-conquer approach)
- Splitting a 128-bit value into two 64-bit chunks
- Branchless arithmetic using bitwise masks

## Code Example
```c
unsigned popcount64(uint64_t v) {
    v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
    return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform fixed-bit manipulations without loops, using SWAR-style packing to reduce 64 bits to 32 bits in one pass.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR parallel bit extraction
- Fixed-mask chain for bit packing
- No branching for deterministic performance

## Code Example

```c
uint32 compressEvenBits64(uint64 x) {
    x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) | ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) | ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) | ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL);
    x = (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
    x = (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
    return (uint32)x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to extract the odd bits instead of even bits?
- What are the trade-offs vs a loop-based approach on various architectures?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to perform a branchless, cross-boundary bitwise scan on a 128-bit value—a common primitive in cryptography and systems programming. It verifies you can correctly propagate carries and parity across the 64-bit lane boundary without branching.

## Key Concepts

- **Parallel prefix (scan) technique**: Each shift-XOR step doubles the bit-range being folded, achieving O(log n) instead of O(n) operations.
- **128-bit right shift implementation**: Requires explicit carry propagation between the hi and lo 64-bit words.
- **Branchless design**: No conditionals ensures constant-time execution, critical for crypto applications.

## Code Example

```javascript
function prefixXor128(x_lo, x_hi) {
  let lo = x_lo, hi = x_hi;
  // 128-bit shift helper: (lo, hi) >> k
  const shift128 = (lo, hi, k) => {
    if (k < 64) return { lo: (lo >>> k) | ((hi & ((1n << k) - 1n)) << (64 - k)), hi: hi >>> k };
    return { lo: hi >>> (k - 64), hi: 0 };
  };
  // Parallel prefix: x ^= x >> {1,2,4,8,16,32,64}
  for (const k of [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64]) {
    const s = shift128(lo, hi, k);
    lo ^= s.lo; hi ^= s.hi;
  }
  return { lo, hi };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for a prefix OR or prefix AND?
- What changes are needed to handle SIMD/vector implementations?
- How does this compare to a naive loop-based approach in terms of cycle count?

