## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform 128-bit two's complement negation and bitwise combination without loops.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit arithmetic via hi/lo halves
- Two's complement negation with carry propagation
- Bitwise AND across full 128-bit value

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 isolateLowestBit128(uint128 x){
  uint128 neg;
  neg.hi = ~x.hi;
  neg.lo = ~x.lo;
  // add 1 to lo, propagate carry
  neg.lo += 1;
  neg.hi += (neg.lo == 0);
  return (uint128){ .hi = x.hi & neg.hi, .lo = x.lo & neg.lo };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test corner cases where trailing zeros span both halves?
- How would you adapt for a wider 256-bit type?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend known 64-bit bit tricks to 128-bit values without branches. Emphasizes portability and optimization.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR parallel bit counting
- Masks and shifts across 64-bit lanes
- Combining results from hi/lo halves

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline unsigned popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  return (unsigned)(x & 0x7F);
}
unsigned countBits128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you use hardware intrinsics if allowed?
- How would you extend to 256-bit types or streaming data?
